That [<s>Brahman</s>], in which all particular <s>upādhis</s> (limiting adjuncts) have set — is pure, stainless, untainted, actionless, tranquil, one, non-dual — knowable [only] through the negation of all particularities as 'not this, not this' (<s>Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad</s> 3.9.26), beyond the reach of all words and concepts. Yet that same [<s>Brahman</s>], through its connection with the <s>upādhi</s> of the internally pure consciousness, becomes omniscient, the Lord (<s>Īśvara</s>), the activator of the common undifferentiated seed of the universe, and is called the Inner Controller (<s>Antaryāmin</s>) by virtue of being the governor. That same [<s>Brahman</s>], assuming the <s>upādhi</s> characterized by the self-identification with the intellect (<s>buddhi</s>) which is the differentiated seed of the universe, is called <s>Hiraṇyagarbha</s>. That same [<s>Brahman</s>], possessing the <s>upādhi</s> of the first body born within the cosmic egg, is called <s>Virāṭ</s>, <s>Prajāpati</s>. That same [<s>Brahman</s>], possessing the <s>upādhis</s> of the elements such as <s>Agni</s> and the rest, is called the deities. Likewise, even in the particular bodily <s>upādhis</s> ranging from <s>Brahmā</s> down to a blade of grass, <s>Brahman</s> acquires the respective names and forms. That one [<s>Brahman</s>] alone, differentiated by the distinctions of all <s>upādhis</s>, is known and conceived in manifold ways by all living beings and by logicians in every manner. The <s>smṛti</s> [states]:
'Some call this <s>Agni</s>, others <s>Manu</s>, others <s>Prajāpati</s>;
some <s>Indra</s>, others <s>Prāṇa</s>, and others the eternal <s>Brahman</s>' (<s>Manusmṛti</s> 12.123) — and so forth. ‖ 3 ‖
